\section{Unknown Does Not Mean Top}

The gradual unknown type $?$ stands for missing static information. In a simple gradual calculus, it is consistent with both $\mathsf{Int}$ and $\mathsf{Bool}$, but those two types are not consistent with each other. Consistency is not transitive and cannot be implemented as an equivalence relation. Nor is $?$ merely a top type: a language can permit a value of type $?$ in an integer context by checking at run time, whereas using a value of ordinary top type in that context normally requires a separate conversion or test.

Jeremy G. Siek, Michael M. Vitousek, Matteo Cimini, and John Tang Boyland's \emph{Refined Criteria for Gradual Typing} separates gradual typing from simply accepting optional annotations. Its static gradual guarantee relates acceptance when annotations are made less precise; the dynamic guarantee relates observations of executions of such variants. In particular, in their setting, weakening annotations should not turn a successful execution into a cast failure. The comparison does not assert that adding precision always succeeds, or that less precise programs take the same path through the checker or have the same cost. A tool that erases all type information and makes no checks at boundaries provides a different runtime contract.

\section{Worked Boundary: The Cast on a Function Is Delayed}

Take a higher-order cast calculus with deep checking. An untyped plugin supplies a dynamically tagged function $f$. The host accepts it at type $\mathsf{Int}\to\mathsf{Int}$. First project to the dynamic function shape $?\to?$; if the value is not callable, report blame immediately. Otherwise one possible wrapper, with polarity-aware blame labels omitted from the notation, behaves like
\[
   \lambda (n : \mathsf{Int}).\
   \mathsf{project}_{\mathsf{Int}}
   \bigl(f(\mathsf{inject}_{\mathsf{Int}}(n))\bigr).
\]
The argument crosses in the opposite direction to the function and is injected into the dynamic representation; the result flows back and is checked. If the plugin returns a string for input zero, importing it can succeed and calling it fails at the result projection. In the reverse direction, exporting a typed function to untyped clients requires checking their inputs. If an untyped client sends a string to an integer function, the client is responsible for that input mismatch, not the typed function. A wrapper can remember the parties and positions at the boundary so the error does not look like a defect in the function body.

The invariant for the host in this model is that every result delivered as a statically typed integer is an integer. Returning the unchecked plugin result would violate it; checking the function tag alone does not establish it. Extending the contract to mutable objects introduces a further issue: a different alias might write after an initial tag test. A deep design must guard interactions or otherwise control updates, not assume the initial test justified all future reads.

\section{Where Costs Accumulate}

Tobias Wrigstad and colleagues' \emph{Integrating Typed and Untyped Code in a Scripting Language} contrasts wrapper-based integration with a design that distinguishes concrete types from \emph{like} types and checks uses of the latter dynamically. The enforcement strategy determines where checks and representation costs land. In the example above a one-time shape check is cheap, but a wrapper may add a call, a tag operation or allocation, and a result test to every iteration of a host loop. Proxy chains can add allocation and indirection, and object identity tests must have specified behavior in their presence. A strategy that checks typed uses instead of wrapping values has different instrumentation and blame behavior; exchanging it for a deep semantics is not a transparent compiler optimization.

Measure crossings as well as aggregate runtime. Compare a fully typed baseline, a fully dynamic baseline, and mixed configurations that put a contract inside and outside the hot loop. Record check counts, allocations, and maximum wrapper depth. An optimizer can fuse compatible coercions or prove a check redundant, but it has to preserve observable failure order and blame according to the language specification. A benchmark with a single cast during startup can miss the dominant cost of an otherwise realistic migration.
